Exercise increases the production of serotonin and dopamine, which are known to improve mood and emotional well-being.
Studies have shown that physical activity can lead to neurogenesis, particularly in the hippocampus, which is linked to memory and learning.
Regular exercise has been associated with enhanced cognitive performance, including better attention and problem-solving abilities.
The release of endorphins during exercise also contributes to a sense of well-being and reduced stress levels.
Long-term physical activity has been linked to structural changes in the brain that support better mental health and cognitive function.